# NO TRAILING SLASH ON ANY PATH A WORKTREE PROVISIONS — this one, `harness/images`,
# `harness/.cache` and `.venv` below. A pattern ending in `/` matches DIRECTORIES ONLY, and a
# worktree's provisioning can put a SYMLINK there, which git does not count as a directory. So
# `node_modules/` did not match `app/node_modules` when it was a link, one `git add -A` in a
# worktree committed it as an absolute path into this Mac, and checking that commit out in the
# MAIN tree replaced the real directory with a link pointing at itself. See D47: it cost the
# 133 MB image mirror before anybody noticed the mechanism.
#
# D47 FIXED TWO OF THE FOUR AND THE OTHER TWO CAME BACK THE SAME WAY (2026-08-30).
# `scripts/worktree-guard.sh` COPIES `harness/.cache` and BUILDS `.venv`, so a directory-only
# pattern is correct for what that script does — and it is correct only for as long as nobody
# provisions them any other way. A session that linked both by hand got exactly D47's state
# back: untracked rather than ignored, one `git add -A` from committing an absolute path into
# one Mac, and `scripts/docs-audit.py` reporting every doc reference to `harness/.cache/` as a
# dangling path because its ignore filter had nothing to match.
#
# So the rule is about the PATH and not about today's provisioning: if a worktree can put
# something at this name, the pattern may not care what kind of thing it is. The precision a
# trailing slash buys is worth nothing here — nothing else in this tree is called `.venv`.
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
node_modules
dist/

# `make up`'s pidfiles and logs. Bare name for the rule stated at the top of this file: this
# is a path a worktree can create, so the pattern may not care what kind of thing is at it.
# Per-checkout by construction — it lives inside the checkout, so a worktree's supervisor
# state dies with the worktree, which is the same property that makes the ports safe (D43).
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
.serve
.claude/settings.local.json

# The same kind of file as the entry above, for the other tool: a machine-local shell
# environment policy (Codex's PKMNSCAN_GATE=on and whatever else a checkout sets), never
# committed for the same reason .claude/settings.local.json is not. .codex/hooks.json, the
# hook roster beside it, IS tracked (D135) — this line is scoped to config.toml alone.
.codex/config.toml
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Glob, not the bare name: an editor that saves as `.env.rtf` or `.env.txt` leaves a
# live key in a path `.env` alone does not match, one `git add -A` from being committed.
.env*
!.env.example
.DS_Store
captures/
output/
*.log

# opsec: never track raw captures or UI renders
captures/
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
harness/images

# D172: the content-addressed photo store, sibling of captures/ runs/ inventory/.
# Bare, no trailing slash, on purpose (see the note at the top of this file) --
# a worktree can put a symlink at this name rather than a real directory.
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
photos

# Derived, reproducible from a public API. The artefact worth committing is the score in
# harness/results/, not the images that produced it or the raw model responses.
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
harness/.cache
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
.venv

# Batch script v2 state. `runs/` is derived — deleting one costs nothing, which is the
# property that makes it safe to re-run anything. `inventory/` is the master store and is
# the opposite: real, local, and never in git. It holds a live map of what is in which box
# and what it sold for, which is not a thing to push to a remote.
runs/
inventory/

# Scope directories: symlinks to a SUBSET of a box, built when the app starts a run over a
# selection rather than a whole box (`server/pipeline_routes.py`). Derived and swept after
# 48 hours, so they are `runs/`-shaped rather than `inventory/`-shaped — but they are listed
# under the opsec rule as well as the derived one, because a link inside one can point at a
# code-card photograph, and a bearer instrument reached through a symlink is still a bearer
# instrument.
.scopes/

# Playwright's own output from `make design-check`. Derived and per-run. The artefact worth
# keeping is the spec, not the record of one execution of it — same rule as harness/.cache
# above. app/package-lock.json is deliberately NOT here: a pinned Playwright version that
# nothing locks is not pinned.
app/test-results/
app/playwright-report/

# AND THE SAME OUTPUT AT THE REPO ROOT, which is where it lands whenever the suite is invoked
# from here rather than from `app/` — `npx --prefix app playwright test --config
# app/playwright.config.ts` resolves `.last-run.json` against the working directory, not the
# config. That is an ordinary way to run one spec, so the artefact is ordinary too; it reached
# a commit on 2026-08-30 because nothing ignored it and `git add -A` does not ask.
#
# Bare, no trailing slash, per the rule at the top of this file. The neighbours above predate
# that lesson and are left alone rather than swept into this change.
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
test-results

# `.claude/launch.json` names a dev port and D43 made the port per checkout, so a tracked
# copy is wrong in every linked worktree — and wrong by pointing the Browser pane at the MAIN
# tree, which is the fault D43 exists to prevent. `make launch-config` writes it from
# `server/ports.py`, the same derivation Vite and Playwright read.
.claude/launch.json

# THE AGENT-ISOLATION WORKTREES. Local state by definition — each one is a checkout the console
# app made for a session, and `git worktree` already knows where they are. Nothing under here
# was ever committable, so this pattern costs nothing at the commit; it is load-bearing for the
# AUDIT. `scripts/docs-audit.py:ignored_paths` exempts gitignored paths precisely so a check
# cannot be "green on the owner's machine and red on a fresh clone" — its own words — and
# without this line three references in docs/DECISIONS.md failed `make check` on any clone that
# had never made a worktree. Found by running the checks on a fresh clone rather than reasoning
# about them.
.claude/worktrees/

# Vale packages sync into styles/ ; only the repo-local style is tracked.
styles/*
!styles/PKMNSCAN/
!styles/config/

# `pipeline/pricehistory.py`'s fetches, cached on disk by `server/pipeline_routes.py`.
# Derived from two PUBLIC mirrors and reproducible by pressing the key again, so it is
# `harness/.cache`-shaped rather than `inventory/`-shaped: deleting it costs a few requests
# and nothing else. Per checkout, because it hangs off `files.home()` (D43).
#
# ANCHORED, and bare. The leading slash keeps it to the repo root so it cannot silently
# swallow `harness/.cache` — which has its own line above and its own reason. No trailing
# slash, per the rule at the top of this file: a worktree can put a link at this name.
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
/.cache

# Built lots. Every one holds a manifest of LIVE CODES — bearer instruments — plus a packing
# slip naming the box they came out of. Derived and deletable the way `runs/` is: the ledger
# under `inventory/` is the truth about what a lot reserved, and a lot directory is only a
# rendering of it that can be rebuilt. Gitignored for `inventory/`'s reason, not a weaker one.
lots/

# The published demo's derived halves. `runs/`-shaped rather than `inventory/`-shaped:
# deleting any of it costs one `make demo`, because `scripts/demo-seed.py` is deterministic
# from a constant seed and the recorder replays a real server over what it wrote. The
# artefact worth committing is the SEED, not the recording it produced — the same rule
# `harness/.cache` is here under.
#
# AND CI REBUILDS ALL OF IT ON EVERY PUSH, which is what makes this safe rather than merely
# tidy: .github/workflows/demo.yml runs seed -> record -> build from source, so the
# published bundle is regenerated from the code it describes and can never be the stale
# recording a committed copy would eventually become.
#
# Bare names, no trailing slash, per the rule at the top of this file.
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
demo
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
app/demo
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
app/public/demo
# ignore-check: bare on purpose, see the note at the top of this file
dist-demo
